WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.220
So, back to kind of the GitHub workflow.
You create a branch, you make commits, you

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.360
have a conversation, and maybe add some more commits. What about when it's time

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.040
to bring your changes back into master
branch? That's when you merge your branch.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:18.539
Now, there's a couple different ways to
do it. In the pull request you can merge

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:23.519
directly on github.com, so you would merge on the remote bringing your branch's

00:00:23.519 --> 00:00:26.849
changes and commits onto this master
branch.

00:00:27.689 --> 00:00:33.329
You can also merge locally with 'git
merge'. Now, merging by itself isn't that

00:00:33.329 --> 00:00:37.890
complex of a topic but there are a lot
of different ways you can merge and some

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.070
problems you can run into. We'll get into
those in some of our next videos.

